 and there are versions of this that date  back decades hundreds of years even  longer maybe I guess I'd have to think  about it without being called the great  replacement but the idea has been around  for a long time this fear of the other  the other is going to come in and  replace us and take our  status in society and maybe take our  riches or maybe take our women right I  mean there's the different versions of  this great replacement idea but it has  been used to stoke nationalist extremist  ideas and it's been around for a long  time here in the United States white  nationalists and other right-wingers  have been pushing this for years in  relation specifically to immigration  policy and usually it relates to Latin  American immigrants and it has  unfortunately become mainstream they've  succeeded at this it's on Fox News all  the time it's not fringe websites that  are talking about this it has infected  what is considered mainstream right-wing  political thinking Fox News host Laura  Ingraham did a segment in August which  we covered basically a sanitized great  replacement segment about legal and  illegal immigrants forcing demographic  change in the United States and  destroying the country by doing it  Tucker Carlson on Fox News very similar  thing numerous segments one in which he  said that brown people coming to the  United States legally becoming citizens  and voting in elections is a form of  election hacking this is fundamentally  it's a great replacement sort of  conspiracy theory another segment in  which Tucker Carlson ranted about  immigrants making our country dirtier  this type of discussion at one point was  not considered serious conversation on  these issues I still would have  disagreed with what the right thought at  the time but they were not overtly  radical in this particular way that I'm  showing you the Institute for Strategic  Dialogue has been studying this and  they've found that these formerly sort  of obscure ideas are now everywhere  Donald Trump references them in speeches  on Twitter it's happening in European  countries among the nationalist parties  it's all over social media particularly  on Twitter even outside of the  president's own tweets the write likes  to say that there's left-wing bias just  implicit in all of our discussions and  on media and they love the example of  Twitter is left-wing because if you  misgender people on purpose  you could get banned this is a joke  compared to the reality that straight up  xenophobic almost phrenologist type  look that up if you don't remember what  that is is now part of the discussion  around many of these issues they have  slick branding they come up with wording  that makes it sound relatively innocuous  if you're not paying close enough  attention but they are pushing extreme  ideas into the gray area of valid  political discussion and it has the  effect that is beneficial for them of  pulling the Overton Window seriously to  the right the rights slick branding has  pushed extreme right-wing ideas into  mainstream discourse they've  successfully integrated them into even  corporate media discussions of many  political issues while at the same time  continuing to shout that there's  inherent left-wing bias in all media and  social platforms this is one of the most  successful disinformation campaigns of  the last thirty years and what we all  need to understand is that even if the  craziest ideas that they espouse aren't  made into reality they're not made into  policy it makes the marginally less  crazy ideas seem middle-of-the-road in  comparison and that's the danger of the  mainstreaming of extremist nationalist  right-wing ideas of the Overton Window  being pushed to the right and of the  creeping normalization that we've  experienced over the last 15 years but  particularly over the last two and a  half under Donald Trump I want to hear  from you about this do you agree with my  assessment and how do you think we fight  it I'm